.TH CLOG_ALERT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_alert \- emit a log line at ALERT level with a backtrace
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_alert(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_ALERT level and picks up __FILE__, __LINE__
and __func__ for you.  After the structured log line it appends a
stack backtrace, the same way clog_error() does:

  ts=... level=ALERT src=file.c:42 func=handle msg="disk full"
  \et#0 ./myapp(handle+0x1a) [0x7f...]
  \et#1 ./myapp(main+0x42) [0x7f...]

ALERT maps to RFC 5424 syslog severity 1, "alert; action must be taken
immediately".  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode each backtrace frame goes out as
a separate syslog message with the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the
original record.

In the order of clog_level_t, ALERT sits between ERROR (severity 3) and
FATAL (severity 0).  It is meant for a condition that an operator has
to look at right away but that does not have to stop the process.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
clog_alert does NOT stop the process, and neither does clog_error; use
clog_fatal when the process must stop.
A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  The capture
itself can fail, on a platform with no execinfo.h or when an allocation
fails for a moment while symbol names are resolved.  The logger then
writes one "backtrace unavailable" marker line in place of the frames
(a marker record in CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode), so you can see that the
backtrace is missing.
The shared write lock covers the backtrace write as well, so the
backtrace always follows its log line without anything in between.
When memory stays short for a while, or the content of a record leaves
little room before the internal buffer cap, a backtrace frame that
cannot be formatted is left out, while a shorter frame after it can
still go out.  An incomplete frame is never written, so every line that
goes out is a complete, well-formed record.  When not a single frame
fits, one "backtrace unavailable" marker line takes their place (a
marker record in CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode), the same marker that a failed
capture gives.  Under the same pressure in CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the
backtrace can fail to fit at all, and a "bt_error":"unavailable" key
then takes the place of "bt", so the omission is visible there too.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clog_alert(lg, "primary database unreachable, failing over");
clog_alert(lg, "disk usage at %d%%, writes may fail soon", pct);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_error (3),
.BR clog_fatal (3),
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3)
